WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.325
Scenario: A caller claiming to be bank support says

2
00:00:03.325 --> 00:00:06.650
a verification code has been sent to your phone

3
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:09.975
and asks you to read it aloud to stop

4
00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:13.299
a suspicious transaction. Safest response: Option A: Hang up

5
00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:16.624
and contact the bank using the number on your

6
00:00:16.624 --> 00:00:19.949
card or official app; never share the code. An

7
00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:23.274
unsolicited caller must not receive an authenticator output; independently

8
00:00:23.274 --> 00:00:26.599
contact the institution. Option B is unsafe because Partial

9
00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:30.293
disclosure can still help an attacker complete an authentication flow.

10
00:00:30.293 --> 00:00:33.618
Option C is unsafe because Knowing personal details does

11
00:00:33.618 --> 00:00:37.312
not authenticate the caller; the code may authorize an action.

12
00:00:37.312 --> 00:00:40.637
Option D is unsafe because Changing the communication channel

13
00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:43.962
does not make disclosure safe. In practice: End the

14
00:00:43.962 --> 00:00:47.656
call and contact the bank through a known official channel.

15
00:00:47.656 --> 00:00:50.981
Review account activity and report the attempted social engineering.

16
00:00:50.981 --> 00:00:54.306
Key takeaway: An OTP is an authenticator output. Do

17
00:00:54.306 --> 00:00:57.631
not disclose it to callers, chat contacts or anyone

18
00:00:57.631 --> 00:00:58.000
else.
